 
796 Problem :-
Given two strings s and goal, return true if and only if s can become goal after some number of shifts on s.
A shift on s consists of moving the leftmost character of s to the rightmost position.
For example, if s = "abcde", then it will be "bcdea" after one shift.
 
Example 1:
Input: s = "abcde", goal = "cdeab"
Output: true

Example 2:
Input: s = "abcde", goal = "abced"
Output: false
 
Constraints:
1 <= s.length, goal.length <= 100
s and goal consist of lowercase English letters.
 
===========================================================================================================================================================================

Solution:-
Intuition
At first, I thought of simulating the shifts one by one (moving the first character to the end). But that approach feels repetitive and unnecessary.
Then I noticed a pattern:
If we take the string s and concatenate it with itself (s + s), all possible rotations of s will appear inside this new string.
So instead of manually shifting, we can simply check if goal exists as a substring of s + s.

Approach
First, check if both strings have the same length.
If not, it's impossible to form goal by rotation.
Create a new string:
word = s + s
Iterate through word and check all substrings of length s.length().
Each substring represents a possible rotation of s.
If any substring matches goal, return true.
If no match is found, return false.

Complexity
Time complexity: - O(n²)
(We check n substrings and each comparison takes up to n time)
Space complexity: - O(n)
(For storing the concatenated string s + s)

Code :-
class Solution {
    public boolean rotateString(String s, String goal) {
        if (s.length() != goal.length()) return false;

        String doubled = s + s;

        return doubled.contains(goal);
    }
}
